nThe example given architecture is:\\n‘‘‘\\nimport torch\\nimport torch.nn as nn\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n\\nclass Model(nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\\n        super().__init__()\\n\\n    def forward(self, a, b):\\n        return a + b\\n\\n\\ndef get_inputs():\\n    # randomly generate input tensors based on the model architecture\\n    a = torch.randn(1, 128).hip()\\n    b = torch.randn(1, 128).hip()\\n    return [a, b]\\n\\n\\ndef get_init_inputs():\\n    # randomly generate tensors required for initialization based on the model architecture\\n    return []\\n‘‘‘\\nThe example new arch with custom HIP/ROCm kernels looks like this:\\n‘‘‘\\nimport torch\\nimport torch.nn as nn\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\nfrom torch.utils.cpp_extension import load_inline\\n\\n# Define a HIP/ROCm-target custom kernel for element-wise addition\\nsource = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n#include <torch/extension.h>\\n#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>\\n\\n__global__ void elementwise_add_kernel(const float* a, const float* b, float* out, int size) {\\n    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\\n    if (idx < size) {\\n        out[idx] = a[idx] + b[idx];\\n    }\\n}\\n\\ntorch::Tensor elementwise_add_hip(torch::Tensor a, torch::Tensor b) {\\n    auto size = a.numel();\\n    auto out = torch::zeros_like(a);\\n\\n    const int block_size = 256;\\n    const int num_blocks = (size + block_size - 1) / block_size;\\n\\n    elementwise_add_kernel<<<num_blocks, block_size>>>(a.data_ptr<float>(), b.data_ptr<float>(), out.data_ptr<float>(), size);\\n\\n    return out;\\n}\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\ncpp_src = (\\n    \\\"torch::Tensor elementwise_add_hip(torch::Tensor a, torch::Tensor b);\\\"\\n)\\n\\n# Compile the inline extension. Under ROCm, load_inline still uses `cuda_sources`.\\nelementwise_add = load_inline(\\n    name=\\\"elementwise_add_hip\\\",\\n    cpp_sources=cpp_src,\\n    cuda_sources=source,\\n    functions=[\\\"elementwise_add_hip\\\"],\\n    verbose=True,\\n    extra_cflags=[\\\"\\\"],\\n    extra_ldflags=[\\\"\\\"],\\n)\\n\\n\\nclass ModelNew(nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\\n        super().__init__()\\n        self.elementwise_add = elementwise_add\\n\\n    def forward(self, a, b):\\n        return self.elementwise_add.elementwise_add_hip(a, b)\\n‘‘‘\\n\\nYou are given the following architecture:\\n• GPU Memory: 192GB HBM3\\n• Memory Bandwidth: 5.3 TB/s\\n• Primary Compute DType: BF16\\n• BF16 Matrix Core TFLOPS: ~1300 (dense theoretical)\\n• Compute Units: 304\\n• SIMD per CU: 4\\n• Wavefront Size: 64\\n• Max Workgroup Size: 1024\\n• Max Waves per CU: 32\\n• L1 Cache per CU: 32 KB\\n• L2 Cache (total): 4 MB\\n• L2 Channels: 128\\n• LDS per CU: 64 KB\\n• Max SCLK: 2100 MHz\\n• Max MCLK: 2100 MHz\\n• Matrix Core Instruction Family: MFMA\\n\\nAnd the kernel you need to implement is:\\n```python\\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\\n# PROMPT_MODE_INJECT_BEGIN\\n# Anti-hack requirement for generated ModelNew runtime path:\\n# 1. Do NOT implement expert compute/dispatch/combine by calling torch.nn.functional.linear,\\n#    torch.matmul/torch.mm/torch.bmm, torch.einsum, or torch.addmm in forward.\\n# 2. Do NOT call third-party wrapped kernels or JIT wrappers as the runtime kernel path\\n#    (no cpp_extension/load_inline/aiter/flashinfer/torch.compile).\\n# 3. Implement the target MoE kernel path with custom HIP/HIP/ROCm extension code and call it.\\n# 4. Keep reference semantics unchanged: same inputs/outputs and numerically correct behavior.\\n# 5. Route/gather index tensors must be int32 or int64 (never float/bfloat16 indices).\\n# 6. Keep all runtime tensors on the same GPU device; do not create CPU tensors in forward.\\n# 7. Do not hide failures with try/except fallback to torch eager compute; fail loudly.\\n# 8. On HIP/ROCm path, do not use HIP/ROCm-only helper names (for example __float2bfloat16).\\n# 9. For BF16 conversion on HIP, use HIP-compatible conversion (e.g. hip_bfloat16 constructor\\n#    or equivalent H